Give you useful web-request evidence such as:

time + client + result + size + method + URL + user + hierarchy + content type

And here's the one mental model I'd carry forward:

PowerShell tells us what's happening on the Windows host; tcpdump tells us what's happening in captured network packets; proxy logs tell us about recorded web requests. Correlating these sources gives us much stronger incident evidence than any one of them alone.

This module is considerably simpler than the PowerShell one so far. The part I'd focus most on next is getting comfortable reading BPF filters, because once src, dst, host, port, tcp/udp, and/or/not become natural, tcpdump questions become much easier.
